<h1>PART 1 &mdash; Point of Contact (POC) User Manual</h1>

<h2>1.1 About the System</h2>
<p>The <strong>Attendance &amp; Workforce Management System (AMS)</strong> is an enterprise-grade, intranet-only web application for managing contract workforce attendance, leave balances, wage calculations, and compliance document generation. The system has zero internet dependency and authenticates via the corporate Active Directory (LDAP).</p>

<h2>1.2 POC Role &mdash; What You Can &amp; Cannot Do</h2>
<p>As a <strong>Point of Contact (POC)</strong>, your job is to record daily attendance for contract workers in your assigned division(s). Your access is strictly scoped â€” you see only the divisions and worker categories assigned to you by the Administrator.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th style='width:55%;'>What a POC <span class='ok'>CAN DO</span></th><th>What a POC <span class='no'>CANNOT DO</span></th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><ul>
      <li>Mark attendance for <strong>allowed days</strong> as set by the Admin (see Section 1.6)</li>
      <li>Re-edit already-saved attendance (but must give a reason)</li>
      <li>View leave balances and attendance ledger for assigned workers</li>
      <li>Send attendance correction remarks or other remarks to Admin</li>
      <li>View and download official notices and circulars</li>
    </ul></td>
    <td><ul>
      <li>Edit Saturday attendance (Saturdays are system-controlled)</li>
      <li>Edit attendance outside the window allowed by Admin</li>
      <li>Override Auto Saturday Cuts</li>
      <li>Manage vendor contracts, employees, or payroll</li>
      <li>Generate official documents or certificates</li>
      <li>View attendance of divisions not assigned to them</li>
    </ul></td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>1.3 Logging In</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Open your web browser and navigate to the AMS URL provided by your system administrator (e.g., <code>http://&lt;server-ip&gt;/attendence/</code>).</li>
  <li>Enter your corporate <strong>Username</strong> (PCNO / Windows domain login ID) and <strong>Password</strong>.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Sign In</strong>. The system verifies your credentials against the corporate Active Directory. Your name, designation, and role are automatically loaded.</li>
</ol>
<div class='critical'>
  <strong>ACCESS DENIED?</strong> Your account may have been revoked by an administrator. Contact your HR department or system manager for assistance.
</div>

<h2>1.4 POC Workspace &amp; Dashboard</h2>
<p>After login you arrive at the <strong>My Workspace</strong> dashboard.</p>
<div class='img-c'><img src='data:image/jpeg;base64,' alt='POC Dashboard' style='width:510pt;height:auto;'/></div>
<p class='img-cap'>Figure 1.4 &mdash; POC User Workspace Dashboard showing the profile sidebar (with accessible divisions &amp; role badge) and 4 module cards</p>
<table>
  <tr><th style='width:22%;'>Component</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Profile Sidebar (Left)</strong></td><td>Shows your official photo, PCNO, Name, Designation, role badge <span class='role-poc'>USER (POC)</span>, and the <strong>list of Accessible Divisions</strong> you are authorized for (e.g., Radar, Microwave, Signal Processing). You only ever see workers belonging to these divisions.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Attendance Card</strong></td><td>Opens the daily attendance marking calendar grid.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Ledger Card</strong></td><td>Opens the monthly leave balance and history tracker.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Remarks Card</strong></td><td>Submit new correction requests or other remarks to Admin; view your sent requests.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Notices Card</strong></td><td>View and download official circulars and announcements.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>1.5 Attendance Grid &mdash; Reading Cell Values</h2>
<p>The Attendance page shows a calendar grid with one column per calendar day and one row per employee. Each cell displays a <strong>value</strong> and optional <strong>label text</strong> below it. Here is how to read every cell type:</p>

<table>
  <tr><th style='width:18%;'>Cell Display</th><th style='width:18%;'>Value Shown</th><th style='width:24%;'>Label Below Cell</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-green'>1</span></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'><strong>1</strong></td>
    <td><em>(none)</em></td>
    <td><strong>Present</strong> &mdash; Employee attended their normal shift that day.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-red'>0</span></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'><strong>0</strong></td>
    <td><em>(none)</em></td>
    <td><strong>Absent</strong> &mdash; Employee did not report to work and no leave type was applied.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-blue'>1</span></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'><strong>1</strong></td>
    <td>Paid</td>
    <td><strong>Paid Leave</strong> &mdash; Employee was absent on authorized paid leave. The day is credited as payable (counts as 1 present day), and 1 day is deducted from their leave balance.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-gray'>0</span></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'><strong>0</strong></td>
    <td>Unpaid</td>
    <td><strong>Unpaid Leave</strong> &mdash; Employee was absent on unpaid leave. The day is not payable; no leave balance deduction.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-yellow'>1</span></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'><strong>1</strong></td>
    <td>Carried</td>
    <td><strong>First Half-Day (Carried)</strong> &mdash; The first half-day is recorded and <em>held</em> as &ldquo;Carried&rdquo;. The day is credited as present (1). No leave deduction yet &mdash; this is pending pairing with a second half-day (which may occur in the same or a future month).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-blue'>1</span></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'><strong>1</strong></td>
    <td>Paired Paid</td>
    <td><strong>Second Half-Day &mdash; Paired as Paid</strong> &mdash; The second half-day has been taken. The pair is resolved as paid; the combined pair counts as 1 payable day and deducts 1 day from the leave balance.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-red'>0</span></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'><strong>0</strong></td>
    <td>Paired Unpaid</td>
    <td><strong>Second Half-Day &mdash; Paired as Unpaid</strong> &mdash; The pair is resolved as unpaid; the combined pair is not payable and no leave balance deduction is made.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-purple'>0</span></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'><strong>0</strong></td>
    <td>Auto Sat-Cut</td>
    <td><strong>Auto Saturday Cut</strong> &mdash; Saturday was automatically cut because the employee was absent on any day in the Mon&ndash;Fri working days of that week. The day is not payable. (Only Admin can override this.)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-orange'>1</span></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'><strong>1</strong></td>
    <td>Holiday</td>
    <td><strong>Public Holiday</strong> &mdash; A declared holiday. The day is credited as present and payable.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style='color:#94a3b8;text-align:center;'><em>empty</em></td>
    <td style='text-align:center;'>&mdash;</td>
    <td><em>(none)</em></td>
    <td><strong>Not Marked</strong> &mdash; Attendance has not been entered for this day yet.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class='note'>
  <strong>Summary of values:</strong> A cell showing <strong>1</strong> means the employee is considered <em>present/payable</em> for that day. A cell showing <strong>0</strong> means <em>absent/non-payable</em>. The label text below the number tells you the leave type where applicable.
</div>

<h2>1.6 Allowed Editing Days (Set by Admin)</h2>
<p>The Administrator controls how many days back a POC can enter or edit attendance. This is configured per category in the Admin settings and cannot be changed by the POC themselves.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Current Month Only (Till Date)</strong></td>
    <td>You can edit any day of the <strong>current calendar month</strong> up to and including <strong>today</strong>. Future dates within the month are locked. Previous months are fully locked.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Rolling N-Day Window</strong></td>
    <td>You can edit dates within the last <strong>N days</strong> from today (e.g., if N = 3, you can edit today, yesterday, and the day before). Dates older than N days or future dates are locked.</td>
  </tr>
</table>
<div class='warn'>
  <strong>Saturday is always locked for POC.</strong> Saturdays are automatically controlled by the system based on the week's attendance pattern. POC users cannot manually edit Saturday cells under any circumstance. If you believe a Saturday was incorrectly processed, submit an Attendance Correction remark to the Admin (Section 1.12).
</div>

<h2>1.7 Marking Attendance Step-by-Step</h2>
<div class='img-c'><img src='data:image/jpeg;base64,' alt='Monthly Attendance Grid' style='width:510pt;height:auto;'/></div>
<p class='img-cap'>Figure 1.7 &mdash; Monthly Attendance Calendar Grid showing cell value badges with label text (Paid, Unpaid, Carried, Paired Paid, Auto Sat-Cut) and the per-employee summary totals column</p>
<ol>
  <li>From your dashboard, click the <strong>Attendance</strong> card (or click Attendance in the top navigation bar).</li>
  <li>Select the required <strong>Month</strong>, <strong>Year</strong>, <strong>Division</strong>, and <strong>Category</strong> from the filter dropdowns at the top of the page.</li>
  <li>The grid loads with all workers in your selected division/category. Each row is one employee; each column is one calendar day.</li>
  <li>Click on the cell for the employee and day you wish to mark. A pop-up/toggle appears with the available options:
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Present (P)</strong> &rarr; Cell shows <span class='cell-demo cell-green'>1</span></li>
      <li><strong>Absent (A)</strong> &rarr; Cell shows <span class='cell-demo cell-red'>0</span></li>
      <li><strong>Paid Leave (PL)</strong> &rarr; Cell shows <span class='cell-demo cell-blue'>1</span> with &ldquo;Paid&rdquo; label</li>
      <li><strong>Unpaid Leave (UPL)</strong> &rarr; Cell shows <span class='cell-demo cell-gray'>0</span> with &ldquo;Unpaid&rdquo; label</li>
      <li><strong>Half-Day (HD)</strong> &rarr; System determines Carried or Paired (see Section 1.9)</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>After marking all required cells, click <strong>Save Attendance</strong> to commit all records to the database.</li>
</ol>
<div class='note'>
  <strong>Auto-Save vs. Manual Save:</strong> Changes are highlighted in the cell but are <strong>NOT</strong> committed to the database until you click <strong>Save Attendance</strong>. If you navigate away before saving, your changes will be lost.
</div>

<h2>1.8 Re-Editing Already-Saved Attendance (Remark Required)</h2>
<p>If you have already saved attendance for a day and then try to <strong>change the value of that cell</strong>, the system detects that a prior entry exists and will prompt you to provide a <strong>reason for the change</strong> before allowing the save.</p>
<h4>How Re-Edit Works:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Click on an attendance cell that was already saved (e.g., change an employee from Present to Absent).</li>
  <li>A <strong>Reason/Remark dialog box</strong> appears: &ldquo;You are changing a previously saved attendance record. Please provide the reason for this change.&rdquo;</li>
  <li>Type a clear reason (e.g., <em>&ldquo;Attendance corrected &mdash; employee was actually absent on this day per site supervisor confirmation.&rdquo;</em>).</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Confirm</strong>. The cell is updated with the new value and the reason is saved as a log entry.</li>
  <li>After making all required changes, click <strong>Save Attendance</strong> as normal.</li>
</ol>
<div class='warn'>
  <strong>All re-edit reasons are logged and visible to the Administrator.</strong> Administrators can view the history of all POC edit remarks per employee per day in the Attendance grid and can clear them if needed.
</div>

<h2>1.9 Half-Day Rules &amp; Cross-Month Pairing</h2>
<p>Half-day leaves follow a strict <strong>pairing system</strong>. Each half-day is stored individually and the system pairs them chronologically across the entire engagement period &mdash; <strong>even across different calendar months</strong>.</p>

<h4>Step 1: First Half-Day &mdash; &ldquo;Carried&rdquo;</h4>
<p>When you mark an employee as <strong>Half-Day</strong> for the first time in a contract stint, the system records it as <strong>&ldquo;Carried&rdquo;</strong>:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Cell displays: <span class='cell-demo cell-yellow'>1</span> with &ldquo;Carried&rdquo; label</li>
  <li>The day is <strong>counted as present (1)</strong> and is payable</li>
  <li>No leave balance deduction at this point</li>
  <li>The half-day is held pending a second half-day</li>
</ul>

<h4>Step 2: Second Half-Day &mdash; &ldquo;Paired&rdquo;</h4>
<p>When the employee takes their <strong>next half-day</strong> (in the same month or any future month within the same contract stint), the system immediately pairs them and prompts you to choose the pairing type:</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Pairing Type Selected</th><th>First Half-Day Cell</th><th>Second Half-Day Cell</th><th>Leave Deduction</th><th>Payable?</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Paired Paid</strong></td>
    <td>Remains <span class='cell-demo cell-yellow'>1</span> Carried</td>
    <td>Changes to <span class='cell-demo cell-blue'>1</span> Paired Paid</td>
    <td>&minus;1.0 day from leave balance</td>
    <td>Yes &mdash; both days count as 1 payable day each</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Paired Unpaid</strong></td>
    <td>Remains <span class='cell-demo cell-yellow'>1</span> Carried</td>
    <td>Changes to <span class='cell-demo cell-red'>0</span> Paired Unpaid</td>
    <td>No deduction</td>
    <td>No &mdash; the second day (Paired Unpaid) is not payable</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class='tip'>
  <strong>CROSS-MONTH PAIRING EXAMPLE:</strong><br/>
  An employee takes a half-day on <strong>25-July-2026</strong> (recorded as &ldquo;Carried&rdquo; in July). They take their next half-day on <strong>03-August-2026</strong>. The system automatically detects the unpaired &ldquo;Carried&rdquo; from July and pairs it with the August half-day. You will be prompted to choose Paid or Unpaid pairing when saving August attendance.<br/>
  <strong>There is NO restriction that both half-days must be in the same month.</strong>
</div>
<div class='note'>
  <strong>Ledger Remark:</strong> After pairing, the Ledger automatically adds a note: <em>&ldquo;2 half days: DD-MMM-YYYY &amp; DD-MMM-YYYY&rdquo;</em> in the employee&apos;s monthly remarks column, regardless of whether the dates span different months.
</div>

<h2>1.10 Saturday Logic Explained</h2>
<p>The AMS system automatically manages Saturday attendance based on the employee&apos;s presence during the Monday-to-Friday working week. <strong>POC users cannot manually set Saturday values.</strong></p>

<h4>How Saturday is Decided:</h4>
<p>After each save, the system evaluates each employee&apos;s Monday-to-Friday attendance for the week containing the Saturday. It checks whether the employee <strong>&ldquo;came&rdquo;</strong> on each of the 5 weekdays. An employee is considered to have &ldquo;come&rdquo; on a day if their attendance is <strong>any of the following</strong>:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Value = <strong>1</strong> (Present)</li>
  <li>Value = <strong>0.5</strong> (Half-day / Carried)</li>
  <li>Leave Type = <strong>Paid</strong> (Paid Leave)</li>
  <li>Leave Type = <strong>Carried</strong> (First Half-Day)</li>
  <li>Leave Type = <strong>Paired Paid</strong> (Second Half-Day Paid)</li>
  <li>Leave Type = <strong>Paired Unpaid</strong> (Second Half-Day Unpaid)</li>
  <li>Day is a declared <strong>Public Holiday</strong></li>
</ul>

<table>
  <tr><th>Week Attendance Scenario</th><th>Saturday Result</th><th>Displayed As</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Employee was present on <strong>ALL 5 weekdays</strong> (Mon-Fri), or any absent day was covered by Paid/Half-Day leave</td>
    <td><strong>Saturday = Present (1)</strong></td>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-green'>1</span></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Employee was genuinely absent (<strong>Absent / Unpaid Leave</strong>) on <strong>even ONE weekday</strong> (Mon-Fri) during the week</td>
    <td><strong>Saturday = Auto Saturday Cut (0)</strong></td>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-purple'>0</span> Auto Sat-Cut</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Employee joined mid-week (join date is after Monday but before Saturday)</td>
    <td>Saturday is also treated as Auto Saturday Cut in that first partial week</td>
    <td><span class='cell-demo cell-purple'>0</span> Auto Sat-Cut</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<div class='critical'>
  <strong>IMPORTANT:</strong> Only an Administrator can override an Auto Saturday Cut (e.g., if the employee had authorized compensatory duty or the absence was later found to be erroneous). If you believe a Saturday cut is incorrect, use the <strong>Attendance Correction remark</strong> (Section 1.12) to notify HR.
</div>

<h2>1.11 Viewing Leave Balances (Ledger)</h2>
<p>Navigate to the <strong>Ledger</strong> from your dashboard card or the top navigation bar. It shows a tabular summary of attendance and leave data for the selected month and your assigned workers.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th style='width:28%;'>Ledger Column</th><th>What It Shows</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Employee / ID</strong></td><td>Worker name and display ID (e.g., S-1, SS-5, US-12)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Opening Balance</strong></td><td>Leave days carried from previous months (or initial grant on first month). Calculated automatically from contract leave credits minus past deductions.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Paid Leaves Taken</strong></td><td>Total authorized paid leave days consumed this month (including paired paid half-days).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Half-Days</strong></td><td>Any single unpaired carried half-days still pending this month.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Unpaid Absences</strong></td><td>Total unpaid absent days this month. Not deducted from leave balance; reduces payable days only.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Saturday Cuts</strong></td><td>Number of Auto Saturday Cuts applied this month.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Closing Balance</strong></td><td>Remaining leave quota = Opening Balance &minus; Paid Leaves Taken</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Present Days</strong></td><td>Total computed present/payable days for the month <strong>after the global adjustment</strong> is applied by Admin (see Section 2.6). This is the value used for payroll.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Final Days</strong></td><td>Final payable days after Admin overrides. If no admin override exists, Final Days = Present Days. If Admin has adjusted a specific employee&apos;s days (e.g., for an error or holiday), Final Days reflects the overridden value.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Remarks</strong></td><td>Auto-generated notes: half-day pairing dates, join/resign events, Saturday overrides, and admin override reasons.</td></tr>
</table>
<div class='note'>
  <strong>Unused leave balances automatically carry forward</strong> as the opening balance for the next month. You do not need to do anything for this to happen.
</div>

<h2>1.12 Sending Remarks to Admin</h2>
<p>Navigate to <strong>Remarks</strong> from your dashboard or top navigation. Use this when you need to flag something to HR or request a change that is outside your POC editing permissions.</p>
<h4>Two Types of Remarks:</h4>
<table>
  <tr><th style='width:35%;'>Remark Type</th><th>When to Use It</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Attendance Correction / Changes</strong><br/><em>(Select this radio button)</em></td>
    <td>Use when you need the Admin to <strong>change a specific attendance value</strong> (e.g., an employee was marked Absent but was actually present, or a past date is locked and needs correction). After selecting this option, you must specify:
      <ul>
        <li>The <strong>Employee</strong> name (selected from dropdown)</li>
        <li>The <strong>Date(s) of concern</strong> (select one or more dates)</li>
        <li>A <strong>Reason / description</strong> of the correction needed</li>
      </ul>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Other Related Remarks</strong><br/><em>(Select this radio button)</em></td>
    <td>Use for any other communication with Admin that does not involve a specific attendance value change (e.g., reporting a general issue, asking a question, noting a pattern of absences requiring HR attention, etc.). Select the employee and dates if applicable, then describe the matter in the Remarks/Reason field.</td>
  </tr>
</table>
<h4>Submitting a Remark:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Click <strong>New Remark</strong> (or the + button) on the Remarks page.</li>
  <li>Select the appropriate radio button: <strong>Attendance correction / changes</strong> or <strong>Other related remarks</strong>.</li>
  <li>Select the <strong>Employee</strong> from the dropdown list.</li>
  <li>Select the <strong>Date(s) of concern</strong> using the date picker and clicking <strong>Add</strong> for each date.</li>
  <li>Type a clear, detailed <strong>Reason / Description</strong>.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Send Remark</strong>. The remark is immediately routed to the Admin&apos;s Remarks Inbox, and a notification badge appears on the Admin&apos;s bell icon.</li>
</ol>
<p>You can monitor the status of your sent remarks (Pending / Resolved / Rejected) in the <strong>My Sent Remarks</strong> table on the same page.</p>

<h2>1.13 Notices &amp; Circulars</h2>
<p>Navigate to <strong>Notices</strong> from your dashboard or navigation. This section shows official organizational announcements, holiday orders, policy circulars, and administrative notices published by HR.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Notices are listed most-recent first.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>View / Download</strong> next to any notice to open or save the attached document (PDF or Word).</li>
  <li>You can only see notices that the Admin has marked as visible to your role.</li>
</ul>

