<!--         PART 2 â€” ADMINISTRATOR MANUAL                            -->
<!-- ================================================================ -->

<h1>PART 2 &mdash; HR / System Administrator Manual</h1>

<h2>2.1 Admin Role &mdash; Full Access Overview</h2>
<p>As an <strong>Administrator</strong> (Role = 1) or <strong>Super Administrator</strong> (Role = 4), you have complete unrestricted access across all organizational divisions, categories, employee records, payroll, settings, and document generation. The key distinction between Admin and Super Admin is that only Super Admins can provision and manage user accounts.</p>
<div class='img-c'><img src='data:image/jpeg;base64,' alt='Admin Dashboard' style='width:510pt;height:auto;'/></div>
<p class='img-cap'>Figure 2.1 &mdash; Administrator Dashboard Page 1 (Daily Operations) showing the Employee, Attendance, Ledger, and Remarks Inbox module cards. Navigate between the 3 pages using scroll, arrow keys, pagination dots, or swipe.</p>
<h2>2.2 Admin Dashboard &mdash; 3-Page Carousel Navigation</h2>
<p>The Admin dashboard is a <strong>swipeable 3-page carousel</strong>. Navigate using: mouse scroll wheel, Left/Right Arrow keys, the pagination dots at the bottom, or swiping on touch screens.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Page</th><th>Title</th><th>Modules Available</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Page 1</strong></td><td>Daily Operations</td><td>Employee Master, Attendance, Ledger, Remarks Inbox</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Page 2</strong></td><td>Periodic Tasks &amp; Notices</td><td>Calculation (Payroll), Documents (Certificates), Notices</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Page 3</strong></td><td>System Administration</td><td>Admin Management, Settings, Service Provider (click to expand: Vendors, Contracts, Wages)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>2.3 Service Provider &amp; Contract Management</h2>

<h3>A. Managing Vendors (Vendors.aspx)</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th style='width:22%;'>Action</th><th>Steps</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Add New Vendor</td><td>Click <strong>Add Vendor</strong>. Enter Agency Name, Master ID, GeM ID, Contact Person, Phone, Address. Click Save.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Edit Vendor</td><td>Click the Edit icon on any vendor row, update fields, and Save.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Deactivate Vendor</td><td>Toggle <strong>Active Status</strong> to Inactive (IsActive = 0). Records are retained for historical referencing. Hard deletion is not permitted.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>View History</td><td>Click any vendor name to see all contract periods and historical worker counts.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>B. Contract Periods (Contracts.aspx)</h3>
<p>Contracts define the operating terms (typically 2 years) between a vendor and a skill category.</p>
<h4>Create a New Contract:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Select <strong>Vendor</strong> and <strong>Skill Category</strong> (Skilled, Semi-Skilled, Unskilled).</li>
  <li>Enter <strong>Start Date</strong> and <strong>End Date</strong>.</li>
  <li>Enter the <strong>GeM Contract Order Number</strong> and <strong>Award Date</strong>.</li>
  <li>Enter the <strong>Initial Leave Balance</strong> to credit to enrolled workers (e.g., 15 days).</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Save Contract</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Extend an Active Contract:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Locate the active contract and click <strong>Extend Contract</strong>.</li>
  <li>Enter the new end date and save. The extension is logged with original and extended dates.</li>
</ol>
<div class='note'>
  <strong>Automatic Contract Closure:</strong> The system automatically closes contracts whose end date has passed. When closed: status becomes &lsquo;Closed&rsquo;, all worker engagements are finalized, employees transition to &lsquo;ContractEnded&rsquo;, and all attendance/wage data from the closed period is <strong>sealed and read-only</strong>.
</div>

<h2>2.4 Employee Master &amp; Career Chaining (Employee.aspx)</h2>
<h4>Adding a New Employee:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Click <strong>Add New Employee</strong> and fill in: Full Name, Division, Skill Category, Contract Period, Vendor, Date of Joining, and optional Qualification/Experience.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Save</strong>. A unique MasterId is assigned and a display ID (e.g., S-1, SS-5, US-12) is generated.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Category Upgrades, Downgrades &amp; Division Transfers:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Locate the employee and click <strong>Edit Engagement</strong>.</li>
  <li>Select the new Category/Division with an effective date.</li>
  <li>The system closes the current engagement stint and opens a new one while retaining the complete historical chain.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Rejoining Former Workers:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Click <strong>Rejoin Employee</strong> and search for the existing profile (even if marked Resigned or ContractEnded).</li>
  <li>Assign the new contract period and click <strong>Confirm Rejoin</strong>. All prior attendance, leave history, and career records are preserved and linked.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Annual Bulk Leave Addition:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Click <strong>Add Bulk Leave</strong>, set the effective date and number of leave days.</li>
  <li>All active workers under the applicable contract receive the credit at once.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2.5 Attendance Control &mdash; Admin Privileges</h2>
<p>Unlike POC users, Administrators can edit attendance for <strong>any date, any employee, and any division</strong> without restriction. Saturdays can also be manually overridden.</p>
<h4>Editing Attendance for Any Date:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Go to <strong>Attendance.aspx</strong>, select Month/Year/Division/Category.</li>
  <li>Click any cell in the grid and select the desired status. Past dates are fully editable.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Save Attendance</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Overriding Auto Saturday Cuts:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Right-click on a Saturday cell showing Auto Sat-Cut.</li>
  <li>Select <strong>Override Saturday Cut</strong>.</li>
  <li>Enter a justification (e.g., &ldquo;Authorized compensatory duty on production line&rdquo;).</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Confirm</strong>. The Saturday changes to Present (1) and the reason is logged.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Declaring Public Holidays:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Click <strong>Holiday Management</strong> from the Attendance page.</li>
  <li>Select the calendar date and enter the Holiday Name (e.g., Independence Day).</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Save Holiday</strong>. All workers on that date are automatically credited as present.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2.6 Global Adjustment &mdash; Adjusting Payable Days</h2>
<p>The <strong>Global Adjustment</strong> is a special admin-only mechanism to add or subtract days from all employees&apos; computed present days simultaneously for a given month &mdash; without modifying individual attendance cells.</p>
<h4>When to Use Global Adjustment:</h4>
<ul>
  <li>A company-wide holiday was declared mid-month and needs to be credited</li>
  <li>A system error affected calculations for all workers in a category</li>
  <li>A bulk correction needs to be applied (e.g., +0.5 for all workers)</li>
</ul>
<h4>Two Levels of Global Adjustment:</h4>
<table>
  <tr><th style='width:30%;'>Adjustment Level</th><th>Scope &amp; Effect</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Global (All Categories)</strong></td>
    <td>A single numeric value added to <strong>every</strong> employee&apos;s present days regardless of category. Stored as <code>EmpID = &lsquo;GLOBAL&rsquo;, Day = 0</code> in the Attendance table.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Category-Specific Global (e.g., GLOBAL_Skilled)</strong></td>
    <td>A separate value added only to employees in a specific skill category. Stored as <code>EmpID = &lsquo;GLOBAL_&lt;TierId&gt;&rsquo;, Day = 0</code>.</td>
  </tr>
</table>
<h4>Formula:</h4>
<div class='code'>PresentDays = (Days Marked Present + Paid Leave days + Saturday credits) + Global Adjustment + Category Global Adjustment</div>
<div class='tip'>
  <strong>Example:</strong> If Global Adjustment = +1.0 for the month of July, every active employee&apos;s Present Days will show +1 day extra in both the Ledger and the payroll Calculation. This is visible as a floating-point adjustment on the attendance grid&apos;s summary row.
</div>

<h2>2.7 Configuring POC Edit Windows</h2>
<p>You control how many past days each POC category group is allowed to edit. This is configured in the <strong>Main Category settings</strong> per category (accessible via Settings or the category configuration panel).</p>
<table>
  <tr><th style='width:30%;'>Setting</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>EditMode = 1</strong><br/>(Current Month Till Date)</td>
    <td>POC can edit any day from the start of the current month up to today. Useful for teams that consolidate attendance at the end of each month.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>EditMode = 0 + EditDaysAllowed = N</strong><br/>(Rolling Window)</td>
    <td>POC can only edit dates within the last N days from today. Useful for teams that enter attendance daily or every few days.</td>
  </tr>
</table>
<p>To change these settings, navigate to the <strong>Main Category configuration</strong>, locate the applicable category, and update <strong>EditMode</strong> and <strong>EditDaysAllowed</strong>. Changes apply immediately to all POC users assigned to that category.</p>

<h2>2.8 Handling Correction Remarks from POC</h2>
<p>When a POC submits a remark, a <strong>notification badge</strong> appears on the bell icon in your top navigation bar and the remark appears in the <strong>Remarks Inbox</strong> (Remarks.aspx, Page 1 of your dashboard).</p>
<h4>Reviewing and Acting on a Remark:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Click the <strong>Notification Bell</strong> or navigate to <strong>Remarks</strong>.</li>
  <li>Unread remarks are highlighted. Click any remark to open the details:
    <ul>
      <li>Submitted by (POC PCNO and name)</li>
      <li>Employee concerned</li>
      <li>Dates of concern</li>
      <li>Remark type (Attendance Correction or Other)</li>
      <li>Description / reason</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>For <strong>Attendance Correction</strong> type remarks:
    <ul>
      <li>Navigate to <strong>Attendance.aspx</strong> and make the required correction.</li>
      <li>Return to Remarks and click <strong>Mark as Resolved</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>For <strong>Other</strong> type remarks, review and click <strong>Mark as Resolved</strong> or <strong>Reject with reason</strong>.</li>
  <li>The POC sees the updated status (Resolved/Rejected) and any admin response note in their sent remarks list.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2.9 Ledger &mdash; PresentDays vs. FinalDays</h2>
<p>The Ledger displays two related but distinct day counts for each employee:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th style='width:22%;'>Column</th><th>How It Is Calculated</th><th>When It Changes</th></tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Present Days</strong></td>
    <td>The raw sum of all payable days from the attendance grid, <strong>plus the Global Adjustment</strong> applied by Admin for that month.<br/>Formula: <code>Î£(P + PL + Saturday credits) + GlobalAdj + CatGlobalAdj</code></td>
    <td>Changes whenever: (1) attendance is edited on the grid, (2) Admin changes the Global Adjustment, or (3) Admin updates a specific employee&apos;s attendance for any reason (e.g., retroactive holiday credit, Saturday override).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Final Days</strong></td>
    <td>The <strong>administratively approved</strong> payable days, stored in <code>CalculationOverrides</code>. If Admin has set a specific override for the employee, Final Days = that override value. If no override: Final Days = Present Days.</td>
    <td>Changes only when Admin explicitly sets a <strong>per-employee override</strong> in the Calculation page (e.g., to correct a billing error or apply a one-time penalty or addition).</td>
  </tr>
</table>
<div class='note'>
  <strong>For payroll and certificate generation:</strong> The system uses <strong>Final Days</strong> for all wage calculations and attendance certificates. If Final Days differs from Present Days, the difference and reason are shown in the Remarks column of the Ledger.
</div>

<h2>2.10 Payroll Calculation (Calculation.aspx)</h2>
<h4>Step 1: Configure Wages</h4>
<p>Before running payroll, verify that wage rates are configured for each skill category in <strong>Wages.aspx</strong>: Basic wage, Variable Dearness Allowance (VDA), total minimum wage, EPF %, and ESI %.</p>
<h4>Step 2: Run Monthly Payroll</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Select <strong>Year</strong>, <strong>Month</strong>, and <strong>Category</strong>, then click <strong>Load Calculation</strong>.</li>
  <li>The system compiles the complete attendance summary: Present Days, Paid Leave, Saturday credits, and Auto Saturday deductions for each worker.</li>
  <li>Enter <strong>Day Adjustments</strong> for individual employees (positive or negative):
    <ul>
      <li><strong>+2.0</strong>: Add 2 days (e.g., retroactive correction for a prior billing shortfall)</li>
      <li><strong>&minus;1.0</strong>: Deduct 1 day (e.g., penalty or administrative correction)</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>The wage formula applied is:
    <div class='code'>Final Monthly Wage = (Final Days + Day Adjustments) &times; Daily Wage Rate</div>
  </li>
  <li>Click <strong>Save &amp; Lock Payroll Statement</strong> to finalize.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2.11 Document Generation Hub (Documents.aspx)</h2>
<p>Generate compliance-ready certificates and letters directly from live data &mdash; no manual template editing required.</p>
<div class='img-c'><img src='data:image/jpeg;base64,' alt='Document Generation Hub' style='width:510pt;height:auto;'/></div>
<p class='img-cap'>Figure 2.11 &mdash; Document Hub showing the Attendance Certificate preview with live data and Export to Word / Export to Excel buttons</p>
<table>
  <tr><th style='width:26%;'>Document Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th style='width:18%;'>Export Format</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Attendance Certificate</strong></td><td>Per-worker attendance tabulation for the month: present days, paid/unpaid leaves, Saturday cuts, and summary remarks. Submitted to vendor for invoice verification.</td><td>.xlsx (Excel)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Satisfactory Certificate</strong></td><td>Formal performance certificate certifying that the vendor supplied manpower satisfactorily for the specified period.</td><td>.doc (Word)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Covering Letter</strong></td><td>Transmittal letter to Accounts/Purchase department authorizing payment of the vendor&apos;s monthly invoice.</td><td>.doc (Word)</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Steps to Generate:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Select the <strong>Document Type</strong> tab (Attendance Certificate, Satisfactory Certificate, or Covering Letter).</li>
  <li>Select <strong>Year</strong>, <strong>Month</strong>, and <strong>Worker Category</strong>.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Load Data</strong>. The system resolves all placeholders (<code>{VendorName}</code>, <code>{ContractNo}</code>, <code>{EmpCount}</code>, etc.) from live database records.</li>
  <li>Select the <strong>Signatory Authority</strong> by PCNO. Name and title auto-populate.</li>
  <li>Toggle column visibility checkboxes if required.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Export to Word</strong> or <strong>Export to Excel</strong> to download. No Microsoft Office installation is required on the server.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2.12 User Administration &amp; Permission Scoping (AdminManagement.aspx)</h2>
<p>Only Super Administrators (Role = 4) can access this page.</p>
<h4>Adding a New User:</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Click <strong>Register New User</strong> and enter the user&apos;s <strong>PCNO</strong>.</li>
  <li>Assign their <strong>System Role</strong>: 0 = POC/User, 1 = Administrator, 4 = Super Administrator.</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Save User</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Assigning Division Scope (for POC Users):</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Open the user&apos;s profile and click <strong>Manage Divisions</strong>.</li>
  <li>Select one or more divisions and click <strong>Save Divisions</strong>. The POC will only see employees from these divisions.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Assigning Category Scope (for POC Users):</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Click <strong>Manage Categories</strong> on the user&apos;s profile.</li>
  <li>Select allowed skill tiers and save. The POC can only mark attendance for these categories.</li>
</ol>
<h4>Revoking Access:</h4>
<p>Change role to <strong>Revoked Admin (2)</strong> or <strong>Revoked User (3)</strong>. The user is blocked at login. All their historical data is preserved.</p>

<h2>2.13 Settings, Audit Logs &amp; Undo Rollback (Settings.aspx)</h2>
<h4>Organization Unit Management:</h4>
<p>Add or rename <strong>Divisions</strong> and <strong>Skill Categories / Tiers</strong>.</p>
<h4>Comprehensive Audit Logging:</h4>
<p>Every data-changing operation in the system is automatically logged in <code>ActionLog</code>, <code>AdminActionLog</code>, or <code>EmployeeActionLogs</code>, capturing:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The performing user&apos;s PCNO and timestamp</li>
  <li><strong>Pre-State</strong>: Complete JSON snapshot of the record before the change</li>
  <li><strong>Post-State</strong>: Complete JSON snapshot after the change</li>
</ul>
<h4>One-Click Transaction Undo (Rollback):</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Go to <strong>Settings</strong> and locate the <strong>Recent Employee Transitions Log</strong>.</li>
  <li>Find the accidental transition (e.g., wrong category upgrade or incorrect division transfer).</li>
  <li>Click <strong>Undo Transaction</strong>. The system reads the pre-state from the audit log and restores the previous record, automatically re-aligning all engagement stints.</li>
</ol>
<div class='warn'>
  <strong>WARNING:</strong> The Undo operation is irreversible in the forward direction. All undo operations are themselves logged. Use only for genuine accidental transactions.
</div>

